    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "Classify each statement. Explain your answer.\n(a) $9t-4$\n(b) $9t-4=23$\n(c) $18-6=12$\n(d) $9t-4=$",
        "solution_text": "(a) $9t-4$ has no equals sign, so it is an **expression**.\n(b) An equals sign joins two expressions, so $9t-4=23$ is an **equation**.\n(c) $18-6=12$ is an **equation**. A pronumeral is not required.\n(d) The RHS is missing. An equals sign alone is not enough, so this is **not an equation**."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "For $22=5p+2$, write down:\n(a) the LHS\n(b) the RHS",
        "solution_text": "Use the position of the equals sign.\n(a) The expression on the left is $22$, so the LHS is $22$.\n(b) The whole expression on the right is $5p+2$, so the RHS is $5p+2$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "(a) Would you simplify or solve $6h+2h-5$?\n(b) Would you simplify or solve $6h+2=18$?\n(c) Mina says every equation has exactly one value for its pronumeral. Use $r+8=8+r$ to explain her error.",
        "solution_text": "(a) There is no equals sign, so this is an expression. **Simplify** it.\n(b) An equals sign joins two expressions, so this is an equation. **Solve** it.\n(c) $r+8=8+r$ is an equation because it has an expression on each side of the equals sign. Adding in either order gives the same total, so it is true for every value of $r$. An equation does not always have just one solution."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "In the expression $4+n$, $n$ can stand for any number.\nWhat value does $n$ stand for in the equation $4+n=11$? Explain why it is a solution.",
        "solution_text": "$n=7$ because $4+7=11$.\nThis value makes the LHS and RHS equal, so it is a solution."
      }
    ],
